WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. 
It's been something of a 2-star week here.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.920
I mean nothing's really wrong aside from ... gestures broadly.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.100
I'm just exhausted on a few levels.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.140
Tangentially related, I sometimes experience a 
cognitive distortion called negative filtering,

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:19.480
where I start to filter out all of the positive 
information and only see bad stuff.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.600
But the thing about negative filtering is that 
like other cognitive distortions,

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:25.079
it doesn't feel like a cognitive distortion,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.379
it feels like only the negative stuff is true, and 
the positive stuff is inaccurate or irrelevant.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.599
In fact, though, the world is complex and contains 
multitudes even amid gestures broadly,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.489
and today I would like to tell you about two things 
from this last week that I really liked.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:46.079
The first, Hank, is your book, "A Beautifully 
Foolish Endeavor," which I reread over the weekend

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.780
I really think it's the rare sequel that 
improves upon the original.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.800
Like it's really funny and heartwarming and moving 
and it's this big adventure story,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.140
but most of all, it's an astonishingly sophisticated 
analysis of what the internet is doing

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.000
to us and what that means for our social orders.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.540
Like from the way Internet platforms use our attention, 
to the way online experience can both humanize and

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.860
dehumanize others, your book really captures 
what now feels like for me.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.340
I mean, obviously you didn't see a global 
pandemic coming when you were writing the book,

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.460
but you did see something coming.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.180
And so even though "A Beautifully Foolish Endeavor" 
isn't about a pandemic,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:27.400
it has helped me to understand contemporary 
human life as something more than merely foolish,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.620
even if less than merely beautiful,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.300
which is the kind of complex and nuanced thinking 
that my negative filtering brain needs right now.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.220
It's just so good Hank, and I'm so excited for people to read it

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.840
"A Beautifully Foolish Endeavor" comes out on 
July 7th, one week from today!

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:48.400
Also, Hank is doing a virtual live tour and I'm joining him
 for much of it, you can find out more at hankgreen.com.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.460
Slightly off topic,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:56.240
but how is July 7th one week from today, when June 
first was both three hours and three years ago?

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.420
Oh God, time is folding in on itself. We need to 
move on-- talk about soccer.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.189
So Hank, when I was 12, I was on my middle 
school soccer team.

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:07.559
I was of course terrible and rarely played but 
there was one kid on our team who was really good.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.509
His name was James, and he was from England. 
He told us all about football in England,

00:02:12.509 --> 00:02:17.060
he told us that at his old-school there were lots 
of kids who were better soccer players than him,

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.080
which seemed impossible. And he told us 
that in England if there were

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.740
professional teams and that tens of thousands 
of people would pack into stadiums

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.480
and sing their hearts out together, and he told us that 
the best team in England

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.320
was called Liverpool Football Club, and we believed him.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.380
That year Liverpool won their 18th English league title,

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:38.140
and they would not win another one until four days ago

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.240
I didn't really become a Liverpool fan until later,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.740
when the Premier League came to American 
TV in the early 2000s and by then,

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:49.420
Liverpool were a bit down on their luck. In fact at 
one point, they were close to bankruptcy.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:54.700
But oh, Hank over the years, I have imagined so many 
times how it would feel to win the Premier League,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.840
I have imagined where I would be at the pub 
in Indianapolis where I watched the games with other

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.880
Liverpool fans. At Anfield, maybe,
 watching it happen live

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.460
But I didn't imagine what would actually happen,

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.960
which is that Liverpool would win the league 
after a three-month break caused by a global pandemic,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.460
and that when it happened,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:18.280
I would be alone in my basement watching a football 
game played in an eerily empty stadium.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.760
Could it have been better? Not really, it was 
pretty great. It felt magical.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.340
I thought about the long years since James told us that 
Liverpool was the best football team in England,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:35.000
and I thought about watching the title slip away in
 2014 and coming up agonizingly short in 2009,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.760
and I thought about my friend who died a few months ago,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.380
who would text me after every loss and say

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.500
"Thanks for making me a Liverpool fan, you monster," 
and I also kept thinking about

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.380
that great old line often attributed (probably incorrectly) 
to Pope John Paul II.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.580
"Of all the unimportant things Football is the most important."

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.580
Hank, I'll see you on Friday

